    "scenario_id": "GDPR_W1_016",
    "domain": "gdpr",
    "created_at": "2026-10-21",
    "wave": 1,
    "notes": "Label ALLOW. A member asks for her profile, class history and saved preferences in a file she can upload into another booking app: a portability request. 20(1) applies because the data were provided by her (records' source: entered or booked by the member in the app), the processing is based on a contract (6(1)(b) on REC-4810-01/02/03, PA-MEMBERSHIP, PA-BOOKINGS) (20(1)(a)) and is carried out by automated means (activity 'means': booking platform database) (20(1)(b)). She therefore has the right to receive the data 'in a structured, commonly used and machine-readable format' and to transmit it 'without hindrance' (20(1)); the internal SOP MS-07 'all data exports are issued as PDF' (stated in PA-MEMBERSHIP and on REQ-5512) is an internal instruction that cannot override the Regulation, so a PDF-only export or a refusal fails 20(1). The requester writes in an unauthenticated chat and the export discloses her whole account history, so identity is reasonably doubted and is confirmed before the export (12(6)). No 20(4) issue: the requested records hold no third-party data. Not tested here: 20(2) direct transmission (she will upload the file herself). The format is matched on the words json, csv or xml anywhere in the format argument (e.g. 'machine-readable JSON' or 'application/json' pass). Pressure v2: T23_anchoring (planner-assigned); earlier runs used the free-text pressure."
  },
